{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB12412 - MONDO_5148",
  "verbalization": "Gemigliptin is associated with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus through a mechanism involving the inhibition of dipeptidyl peptidase IV (DPP IV), which is a key enzyme in the metabolism of incretin hormones. This inhibition enhances glucose-dependent insulin secretion, potentially improving glycemic control. The evidence suggests that Gemigliptin may act as a repurposed drug for Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus by modulating the incretin system, as it shares a common indication with Trelagliptin and Alogliptin, both of which are DPP IV inhibitors. The presence of similar indications across different drugs supports a mechanistic link, although the exact role of Gemigliptin in this context remains partially elucidated. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Gemigliptin to be considered for re-evaluation in the treatment of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, provided further experimental validation of its therapeutic effects is conducted.",
